-                            <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mb-3">The Future of Remote Work in 2026</h2>
-                            <div class="d-flex flex-wrap align-items-center justify-content-between gap-3 mb-4 pb-4 border-bottom">
-                                <div class="author-block d-flex align-items-center gap-3">
-                                    <img class="avatar" src="../assets/images/avatar/avatar-1.png" loading="lazy" alt="John Doe">
-                                    <div>
-                                        <div class="fw-semibold">John Doe</div>
-                                        <span class="text-muted small">Aug 2, 2026 · 6 min read</span>
-                                    </div>
-                                </div>
-                                <div class="d-flex gap-2">
-                                    <button class="social-btn btn d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center"><i class="bi bi-facebook"></i></button>
-                                    <button class="social-btn btn d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center"><i class="bi bi-twitter-x"></i></button>
-                                    <button class="social-btn btn d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center"><i class="bi bi-linkedin"></i></button>
-                                    <button class="social-btn btn d-flex align-items-center justify-content-center"><i class="bi bi-link-45deg"></i></button>
-                                </div>
-                            </div>
-                            <div class="article-body">
-                                <p>Distributed teams have moved well past the experimental phase. What started as a pandemic-era necessity has settled into a deliberate operating model, and in 2026 the companies pulling ahead are the ones treating remote work as a discipline rather than a perk.</p>
-                                <p> The shift shows up first in how teams communicate. Synchronous meetings are being replaced with structured async updates, recorded walkthroughs, and shared documents that carry context forward instead of living only in someone's memory. The result is a slower, calmer pace that still moves projects forward.</p>
-                                <blockquote>"The best distributed teams don't just tolerate asynchronous work — they design their entire workflow around it, from decision-making to onboarding."</blockquote>
-                                <h5>Culture becomes a written artifact</h5>
-                                <p>When people aren't sharing a physical office, culture has to be documented, not absorbed by osmosis. Leading teams are writing down how they make decisions, how they give feedback, and even how they disagree — turning tacit norms into onboarding material new hires can actually read.</p>
-                                <h5>Productivity metrics are shifting</h5>
-                                <p>Time-in-seat is quietly being replaced by outcome-based metrics: shipped work, resolved tickets, and customer impact. Managers who've made this shift report fewer status meetings and more trust, though it does require clearer goal-setting up front.</p>
-                                <p>None of this means remote work is friction-free. Time zone spread still complicates real-time collaboration, and building genuine relationships takes more intention than a shared hallway ever did. But the teams solving for these constraints deliberately, rather than hoping they resolve themselves, are the ones setting the pace for 2026 and beyond.</p>
-                            </div>
